INTERPRETATION (kps-S4): the k>=3 covering S3 floor (M=2/21, M*14=1.33) is LOOSER than the
global covering floor 2/23 (M*14=1.22, achieved at k<=2). Tightness is ALWAYS driven by an
ADJACENT SMALL pair {a,a+1} (or near-adjacent) binding at a small-denominator crossing k/(a+b);
the cluster never binds tightly. So the genuinely-tight covering sets are k<=2 (PROVED slice);
k>=3 has slack >= 2/21. The binding-large-runner cases (M~0.10) always have the large binder
== 0 mod 7 (covering-forced multiple of 7/14) — the 7-adic mechanism — but bind comfortably.
